    "likes": 0,
    "description": `We often talk about the future of AI in terms of what it will be able to do.<br />

But I think there is a more important question:<br />
<strong>What happens when AI becomes capable of doing almost every job?</strong><br /><br />

Imagine a future where AI can write software, analyze financial data, create designs, teach students, drive vehicles, provide customer support, and perform many physical tasks—faster, cheaper, and potentially better than humans.<br />
At that point, companies will have a strong reason to automate.<br />
But if millions of people are replaced, another problem appears:<br />
<strong>What happens to the people who no longer have jobs?</strong><br />
<strong>Human Reserved</strong><br />
This is where I believe governments may eventually step in.<br />
They could create a concept I would call <strong>“Human Lifes Matter”</strong>—certain jobs or responsibilities that must remain available to humans, even when AI is technically capable of doing them.<br />
Bill Gates has discussed a similar idea: society may deliberately preserve certain types of work for humans rather than allowing automation to completely eliminate the human role.<br />
The reason wouldn't necessarily be that humans are better at the job.<br />
It could be because 
<strong>human participation itself has value.</strong><br />
A teacher, doctor, caregiver, judge, or other professional may still need to be human—not because AI cannot perform the task, but because society may decide that some responsibilities should remain human.<br /><br />
<strong>The Bigger Question</strong><br />
There is also an economic reason to think about human employment.<br />
If people lose their jobs, they lose income.<br />
If they lose income, they spend less.<br />

And when millions of people stop spending, businesses can eventually lose customers.<br />

The basic cycle is simple:<br />

<strong>People work → earn → spend → businesses earn → businesses produce → people work.</strong><br />

So if AI eventually performs most of the work, governments may have to think beyond productivity.<br />

They may have to think about <strong>how humans continue participating in the economy.</strong><br /><br />

<strong>The Future</strong><br />

Maybe the future won't be about stopping AI.<br />

Maybe it will be about deciding <strong>where AI should replace humans and where humans should remain.</strong><br />

AI could become responsible for the work it does best, while governments protect certain areas of human employment.<br />

And perhaps one day, <strong>“Human Reserved”</strong> won't sound unusual at all.<br />

It may simply become another part of how society works.<br /><br />

<strong>AI could do the job.<br />
But society may decide that humans should.</strong>`
